th doctors is by the  internet all of the doctors are asking  for internet they earn less than 40  grand a year and are disappointed they  don't qualify for the newly expanded  affordable internet program well for one  thing i could get my bottom teeth fixed  connecting families 2.0 is now for  low-income families and seniors who  receive either the maximum canada child  benefit or the maximum guaranteed income  supplement at twenty dollars a month the  government is promising internet speeds  5 and 10 times faster than the previous  10 plan which people can still access if  they wish data doubles to 200 gigabytes  per month  14 internet service providers are on  board we frequently hear that people in  canada pay some of the highest prices in  the world for internet and cell phone  connectivity advocacy groups applaud the  move but are calling for expansions to  the eligibility list and the data limit  for the internet of 2022 that simply  isn't enough it deepens the digital  divide we would certainly like to see  this expanded to anyone who qualifies  for the gis at all  and of course on a go forward basis to  make sure that the quality of the  internet that they're getting is also  going to be  good experts say the number of seniors  who qualify is not big and exactly how  many people will be helped is not clear  anyone eligible will be sent a letter  those letters have already gone out but  the government isn't saying how many  were issued abigail beeman global news  ottawa  bracing for the federal budget ahead the  potential billions in new spending  you're watching global national  deputy prime minister and finance  minister christopher freeland is putting  the finishing touches on the latest  federal budget it is set to be delivered  on thursday with plenty of demands for  new spending and investments in  everything from housing to dental care  to defense david aiken looks at what's  going into the equation of the upcoming  budget  10 a day child care right a